.PP
Implements \fBcsmp::MathOperatorLHS< dim, Element >\fP\&.
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELL> void \fBcsmp::NumIntegral_BT_D_B_dV\fP< dim, CELL >::ComputeContribution (const CELL< dim > & e)\fR [final]\fP, \fR [override]\fP, \fR [virtual]\fP"
Computes the 2D spatial integral over the matrix product:

.PP
[B]^T [JI]^T [D] [B] [JI] detJ

.PP
as the elements lefthand contribution to the global stiffness matrix\&. The integration is performed numerically using a 3 Gauss-Point scheme in the case of the quadratic triangular finite element\&. 
.br
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
A reference to element, the contribution of which is to be aquired and the time-increment over which the deformation shall occur\&.

.PP
The result of the computation is returned into the base class protected member matrix [C]\&. 
.br
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
In linear elasticity computations\&. 
.PP
Reimplemented from \fBcsmp::MathOperatorLHS< dim, Element >\fP\&.
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELL> void \fBcsmp::NumIntegral_BT_D_B_dV\fP< dim, CELL >::GetOperands (const CELL< dim > & e)\fR [final]\fP, \fR [override]\fP"
While Poisson's ratio must be an element variable, this method allows for Young's modulus to be a node variable\&. Thus, a continuous loss of strength can be modeled\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class CELL> void \fBcsmp::NumIntegral_BT_D_B_dV\fP< dim, CELL >::PlaneStress (bool yes_no = \fRtrue\fP)"
default is plane strain

.PP
Allows to toggle between plane stress and plane strain computations in two-dimensional models\&. Clearly, this method only has an effect in 2D calculations and the chosen computation must match the preceding Algorithm\&. 
.br
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
A boolean variable specifying whether the material property matrix used shall be initialized for plane strain or plane stress\&. 
.br
.SH "Messages"
.PP
If the method is called in a 3D calculation, the user is warned that it will have no effect\&. 

